The probability of rolling a $1$ on a fair die is $\frac{1}{6}$ .  So if you roll the two equal probability dice, the probability that the first die is a $1$ and the second die is a $1$ is $\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}=\frac{1}{36}$ .  If the first die is a $1$, then there are $5$ different outcomes on the second die.  Two of them is a $1$ and $3$ of them are not $1$ .  Thus, after rolling a $1$ on the first die, the probability that the second die does not match is $\frac{5}{6}$ .  Thus, the probability that both dice roll are a $1$ is $\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{5}{6}=\frac{5}{36}$ .  There are $11$ different outcomes possible if at least one is a $1$ .  Of these, $5$ are for the die that can be a $1$ and the second die can not .  Of the $30$ outcomes when the second can be a $1$, only $5$ are counted twice since we already counted both dice.  All together, there are $(11)(5)+30=85$ .  Thus, the probability of each of the $85$ outcomes is $\frac{1}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}=\frac{1}{36}$ .  Thus, the percentage of this outcome with the two dice both being a $1$ is $(85)(\frac{1}{36})\approx23.6$ or $23.6$ percent.
